{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Your counselors.</b> A strange word, for the singular and plural markers are joined together. Similar is והפלא יי את מכותך (Deuteronomy 28:59). And if it is the reverse:" "<b>Those who gaze at the heavens.</b> It has no [phonetic] defect, and its meaning is 'those skilled in the heavens,' namely the astrologers. Some derive it from the root ברר (to be clear), the reason being that they cannot know the parts of the hour unless the air of the heavens is clear and pure. This is a far-fetched path, for the heh is primary, and it is on the pattern of שומרי החומות (Song of Songs 5:7)." "<b>Those who declare [things] for the new moons.</b> Such is their science, for they do not seek to know all phenomena in the air and in the lands, except from the time of the conjunction of the luminaries, for this is the core of their craft. This is the meaning of 'for the new moons.'" "<b>From what will come upon you.</b> It does not attach to 'for the new moons,' but only to the word ויושיעוך (and may they save you). And if it is distant, similar is אשר אתם יורשים אותם את אלהיהם (Deuteronomy 12:2)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {0 "The comment cuts off with 'if it is the reverse,' leaving the alternative unexplained; translated as is."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:58:30.601057927Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}